# Noor — Architecture Rules

These rules are non-negotiable for any code generated or edited in this
repository. They exist to keep the app privacy-safe, maintainable on a
150-line-per-file limit, and accessible.

## 1. Offline-first, always
- Zero ad SDKs. Zero third-party analytics or telemetry SDKs.
- Zero remote network calls anywhere in `lib/`. No `http`, `dio`, sockets,
  or remote fetch of any kind. The app must function with no INTERNET
  permission in the Android manifest.
- All data (Quran text, azkar, prayer calculation tables) ships bundled
  in `assets/` or the local encrypted database — never fetched at runtime.

## 2. File size limit
- No single `.dart` file may exceed 150 lines of code.
- If a file grows past that, split it (e.g. extract a widget, extract a
  mapper, extract constants) rather than requesting an exception.

## 3. Decoupled layers
- UI (`presentation/`) never calls `DatabaseHelper`, SQL, or calculation
  logic directly.
- UI only talks to a Cubit/Bloc. The Cubit talks to a repository
  (`data/`). The repository is the only layer allowed to touch
  `DatabaseHelper` or perform math (e.g. prayer time / qibla calc).

## 4. Accessibility
- Every interactive widget (buttons, toggles, list items, sliders) must
  be wrapped in an explicit `Semantics()` node with a meaningful
  `label`/`hint` for VoiceOver and TalkBack — not just relying on
  default widget semantics.

## 5. Haptics discipline (Tasbih-specific)
- `HapticFeedback` is only ever called from `HapticService`. Widgets
  and Cubits call `HapticService`, never `HapticFeedback` directly.
- Standard tap = light impact. Counts of 33 / 66 / 100 = double heavy
  pulse via `HapticService.onCount()`.
